/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For a state function F of two variables x and y, dF = M dx + N dy is exact.
   - Symbols: U internal energy (J), H enthalpy (J), A Helmholtz energy (J), G Gibbs energy (J), T absolute temperature (K), S entropy (J/K), P pressure (Pa), V volume (m³).
   - Each line is one Maxwell relation.
   - The dG relation is the workhorse for entropy changes with pressure at fixed temperature.
   - S is entropy (J/K), P pressure (Pa), V volume (m³), T temperature (K); the subscript T means temperature is held constant.
   - At constant T, integrate from P1 to P2.
   - H is enthalpy (J); the other symbols are as above.
   - The Joule–Thomson coefficient μJT = (∂T/∂P)H follows from the same enthalpy slope.
   - The variable held constant is part of the derivative.
   - The dA relation can be more convenient when volume data are available: at constant T, ΔS = ∫(∂P/∂T)V dV.
*/
