/* book Worked example 2: Q: Liquid water at 25 °C (298.15 K) is pumped from 1 bar to 100 bar. Its molar volume is V = 18.07 × 10⁻⁶ m³ mol⁻¹ and its thermal expansion coefficient α = 2.573 × 10⁻⁴ K⁻¹; take both as constant. Use Maxwell relations to find ΔS and ΔH, and compare with the steam-table values (−0.0469 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ and 164.5 J/mol).
   1. Maxwell relation from dG: (∂S/∂P)T = −(∂V/∂T)P = −Vα.
   2. ΔS = −VαΔP = −18.07 × 10⁻⁶ × 2.573 × 10⁻⁴ × 99 × 10⁵ = −0.0460 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.
   3. Enthalpy slope: (∂H/∂P)T = V − T(∂V/∂T)P = V(1 − Tα).
   4. 1 − Tα = 1 − 298.15 × 2.573 × 10⁻⁴ = 1 − 0.0767 = 0.9233.
   5. ΔH = V(1 − Tα)ΔP = 18.07 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.9233 × 99 × 10⁵ = 165.2 J/mol.
   6. Compare: the steam-table values are −0.0469 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ and 164.5 J/mol, within 2 % and 0.4 %. The simple estimate ΔH ≈ VΔP = 178.9 J/mol would be 9 % high, because it misses the −Tα term.
   Answer: ΔS = −0.0460 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ and ΔH = 165.2 J/mol, close to the table values −0.0469 and 164.5. */
