/* book Worked example 2: Q: Find Z, GR, HR, SR and φ for CO2 at 350 K and 20 bar with the virial equation and the Abbott correlation. Data: Tc = 304.2 K, Pc = 73.83 bar, ω = 0.224.
   1. Reduced state: Tr = 350/304.2 = 1.1506; Pr = 20/73.83 = 0.2709.
   2. B⁰ = 0.083 − 0.422/1.15061.6 = −0.2542; B¹ = 0.139 − 0.172/1.15064.2 = 0.0436; BPc/RTc = −0.2542 + 0.224 × 0.0436 = −0.2444.
   3. Z = 1 + (BPc/RTc)(Pr/Tr) = 1 − 0.2444 × 0.2709/1.1506 = 0.9425.
   4. GR/RT = ln φ = Z − 1 = −0.0575, so φ = 0.944 and GR = 8.314 × 350 × (−0.0575) = −167.5 J/mol.
   5. Slopes: dB⁰/dTr = 0.675/Tr2.6 = 0.4687; dB¹/dTr = 0.722/Tr5.2 = 0.3482.
   6. HR/RTc = Pr[B⁰ − Tr dB⁰/dTr + ω(B¹ − Tr dB¹/dTr)] = 0.2709 × [−0.2542 − 0.5393 + 0.224 × (0.0436 − 0.4006)] = −0.2366, so HR = −0.2366 × 8.314 × 304.2 = −598.4 J/mol.
   7. SR/R = −Pr(dB⁰/dTr + ω dB¹/dTr) = −0.2709 × (0.4687 + 0.0780) = −0.1481, so SR = −1.231 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.
   8. Check: HR − TSR = −598.4 + 350 × 1.231 = −167.5 J/mol = GR. An accurate equation of state for CO2 gives Z = 0.940, HR = −604 J/mol, SR = −1.243 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ and φ = 0.944, within 1 %.
   Answer: Z = 0.9425, φ = 0.944, GR = −167.5 J/mol, HR = −598.4 J/mol, SR = −1.231 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. */
